# Every tracked file in this repo is Unix content that bash reads: the `shell/`
# layers `.zshrc` sources, the `apps/` scripts that exec through
# `#!/usr/bin/env bash`, and the configs deployed verbatim into `$HOME`. There is
# no Windows-native script here to want the other convention — `git ls-files
# '*.ps1' '*.bat' '*.cmd'` is empty — so this is one blanket rule rather than a
# per-extension list, which also covers the `apps/common/` executables that carry
# no extension at all.
#
# The Windows machine sets `core.autocrlf = true`
# (`configs/os/windows/.config/git/windows.gitconfig`), which is correct for the
# Windows-native projects that side of the box exists for and fatal here. Bash
# cannot source a file whose lines end `\r`, and cannot exec
# `#!/usr/bin/env bash^M`. That machine also deploys by copy from its own
# checkout, so a CRLF checkout is a CRLF `~/.bashrc` and a CRLF
# `~/.local/shell/*.sh`. `eol=lf` is what scopes this repo out of that setting;
# the Windows-native repos beside it keep it.
#
# `.editorconfig` declaring `end_of_line = lf` does not do this job. Editors read
# it, git does not, and the conversion that breaks these files happens at
# checkout.
#
# `text=auto` rather than a bare `text` so git keeps detecting binaries itself;
# nothing tracked here is one today, and a future font or image needs no entry.
* text=auto eol=lf
